target/
!.mvn/wrapper/maven-wrapper.jar

### Executor per-run working directories (application.properties: featuresFolder)
runs/

### Page object and steps variants with HARDCODED TEST DATA — real CIF, userid
### and passwords. CLAUDE.md has always said "both .gitignore and .dockerignore
### guard it"; .dockerignore did, THIS FILE DID NOT — the only mention here was
### a comment on another rule, so a `git add .` or a select-all in the IDE's
### commit dialog would have committed live bank credentials. Found 2026-10-06.
DO_NOT_COMMIT/
**/DO_NOT_COMMIT/

### Playwright MCP's browser profile: holds a logged-in bank session cookie.
### Never commit — same reasoning as DO_NOT_COMMIT.
.mcp-browser-profile/

### NOT ignored, on purpose: .mcp.json, which sits right next to the directory
### above and looks like the same kind of local-tooling file. It is not. The
### ORCHESTRATOR reads it at startup — application.properties points
### spring.ai.mcp.client.stdio.servers-configuration at it — so a checkout
### without it has no "Ajan", and ignoring it would mean every machine that
### runs the orchestrator has to be told to hand-write one.
###
### The objection to committing it is that it carries an absolute path
### (C:/tools/playwright-mcp/...), which is the shape that made .idea/misc.xml
### flip back and forth between two machines forever. That is answered by
### fixing the path rather than by ignoring the file: the tool is installed at
### the SAME location on the local machine and on the VDI (confirmed writable
### there, 2026-10-15), so there is no per-machine value left to fight over.
### If a third machine ever cannot use C:/tools, the fix is an .mcp.json.example
### plus a local override — NOT a different path committed here.

### Node tooling is installed OUTSIDE this repo (see .mcp.json), but a stray
### `npm install` here would otherwise commit thousands of files.
node_modules/

### The React UI's BUILD OUTPUT. Generated by `npm run build` in
### uptest-orchestrator/src/main/frontend (vite writes it here), served by
### Spring from the classpath at /ui/.
###
### It WAS committed, deliberately, so the VDI could run the jar without
### needing npm. That trade stopped being worth it: the bundle is ~265 kB and
### its sourcemap ~1.2 MB, both rewritten under a new content hash on every UI
### change, so a two-line fix landed 1.5 MB of churn and the old files had to
### be deleted in the same commit. Reviewing a UI change meant reading past a
### minified diff nobody can read.
###
### THE COST OF THIS RULE, stated plainly so nobody is surprised by it:
### building the jar no longer builds the UI. Maven has no frontend plugin
### (checked: uptest-orchestrator/pom.xml declares compiler, surefire and
### spring-boot only), so `mvn install` on a fresh checkout produces a jar with
### NO /ui/ in it — and since UiRoutes redirects "/" to "/ui/", the whole app
### looks dead. The order is: npm run build, THEN mvn install.
###
### NOTE: these files are already TRACKED, and git applies no ignore rule to a
### tracked file. The rule below does nothing until you also run, once:
###     git rm -r --cached uptest-orchestrator/src/main/resources/static/ui
uptest-orchestrator/src/main/resources/static/ui/

### npm's failure log. It is written into the CURRENT directory, so a failed
### install under src/main/frontend leaves it there — and that directory's own
### .gitignore covers node_modules/ and nothing else. Installs there go through
### the corporate proxy, which is exactly the setup that fails often enough for
### this file to appear. It records the registry URL and every resolved
### dependency path.
npm-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*

### Corporate CA certificates: site-specific, distributed like the VDI trust
### bundle, not committed. See docker/certs/README.md.
docker/certs/*.crt
docker/certs/*.cer
docker/certs/*.pem

### Keys, keystores and truststores ANYWHERE in the tree, not just under
### docker/certs/. .dockerignore has guarded these since it was written
### (**/*.p12, **/*.jks, **/*.key, **/*.pem) and this file did not — the same
### asymmetry that let DO_NOT_COMMIT/ go unguarded here for months. The path
### that makes it concrete: fixing Java's PKIX failures on the VDI means
### exporting the Windows-ROOT bundle into a truststore, and the obvious place
### to drop that file is next to the pom you are running.
*.jks
*.p12
*.pfx
*.keystore
*.truststore
*.key
cacerts

### Local compose overrides (proxy, registry, host paths). The bare `.env` was
### the only form covered; .dockerignore already took `.env*`, and the variants
### people actually write (.env.local, .env.vdi) were being offered for commit.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

### Spring's local profile override — by convention the file you put the real
### datasource URL, proxy host or credentials in so you do not have to edit the
### committed application.properties. .dockerignore keeps it out of an image;
### keep it out of history too.
**/application-local.properties

### Claude Code — local tooling config, per-machine, not project content
.claude/
.claude/**
!**/src/main/**/target/
!**/src/test/**/target/
.kotlin

### IntelliJ IDEA ###
### workspace.xml is per-developer UI state — open tabs, window layout, run
### configurations, the local history of what you looked at. Committing it
### means every checkout fights over it.
.idea/workspace.xml
.idea/modules.xml
.idea/jarRepositories.xml
.idea/compiler.xml
.idea/libraries/

### Machine-specific: it records project-jdk-name ("temurin-21" here) and the
### language level. The VDI's JDK has a different name, so IntelliJ rewrites
### this file on every open and the two machines flip it back and forth forever.
### Nothing in it is worth sharing — the BUILD's Java version comes from
### maven.compiler.release in pom.xml, not from here.
###
### NOTE: this file is already TRACKED, and git applies no ignore rule to a
### tracked file. The rule below does nothing until you also run, on EACH
### machine (the two index files are separate):
###     git rm --cached .idea/misc.xml
.idea/misc.xml

### IntelliJ's own compiler output. misc.xml points at it
### (<output url="$PROJECT_DIR$/out" />), so a Build > Build Project — rather
### than Maven — fills it with hundreds of .class files that then show up as
### unversioned. The Maven build uses target/, which is ignored on line 1.
out/

### Kept ON PURPOSE, do not add them here: .idea/.gitignore (it is what ignores
### workspace.xml and friends), .idea/vcs.xml (four lines, never changes) and
### .idea/encodings.xml (project-wide UTF-8 — load-bearing for a codebase this
### full of Turkish text, and for .properties files, which IntelliJ otherwise
### reads as ISO-8859-1).

*.iws
*.iml
*.ipr

