### Windows ###
### The Mac rule above was here and its Windows counterpart was not, on a
### checkout that lives on a Windows SMB share. Explorer writes both of these
### by itself — no one runs a command to create them — and desktop.ini in
### particular appears the moment a folder gets an icon or is marked read-only
### by the share.
Thumbs.db
ehthumbs.db
desktop.ini
$RECYCLE.BIN/

### H2 run-history database (application.properties: spring.datasource.url)
# Normally NOT in the checkout at all: the database lives under LOCALAPPDATA,
# on the VDI's local disk, because the checkout itself sits on an SMB share and
# H2 must not keep a database file there.
#
# These rules cover the two cases where it still lands here: the fallback path
# when LOCALAPPDATA is unset, and anyone pointing SPRING_DATASOURCE_URL at the
# repo for an afternoon. It holds run history and analyst intent text.
#
# Note *.trace.db especially — it is H2's error log and it quotes failing SQL
# INCLUDING BOUND PARAMETER VALUES, which for this schema means scenario text.
data/
*.mv.db
*.trace.db
*.lock.db

# graphify knowledge graph output (local only, regenerate with /graphify .)
graphify-out/

### JVM crash dumps. Note where they land: the WORKING DIRECTORY, not target/,
### so line 1 does not catch them. Every scenario starts a Playwright node
### driver and a browser out of this JVM, which is the kind of native-boundary
### work that produces an hs_err file when it goes wrong. They are large and
### they quote the full command line and environment.
hs_err_pid*.log
replay_pid*.log

### Merge and patch leftovers. Harmless, but a committed *.orig is a second
### copy of a file that silently stops tracking the real one.
*.orig
*.rej
